## Why This Is Asked
Tests advanced distributed systems concepts: sagas, event sourcing, CQRS, and failure handling at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Saga orchestration vs choreography trade-offs
- Event sourcing with immutable event streams
- Idempotency via correlation IDs and deduplication
- Compensating actions for distributed transactions
- CQRS separation for read/write optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
// Saga orchestrator pseudo-code
class OrderSaga {
  async handleOrderCreated(event) {
    const { orderId, items, total } = event.data;

    const inventoryResult = await this.reserveInventory(orderId, items);
    if (!inventoryResult.success) {
      await this.compensateWithOrderCancelled(orderId);
      return;
    }

    const paymentResult = await this.authorizePayment(orderId, total);
    if (!paymentResult.success) {
      await this.releaseInventory(orderId, items);
      await this.compensateWithOrderCancelled(orderId);
      return;
    }

    await this.createShipping(orderId, event.data.shippingAddress);
    await this.completeOrder(orderId);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test end-to-end saga correctness across services?
- What tracing and metrics would you collect for correlation across microservices?
- How would you handle saga timeout scenarios and cleanup?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical mastery of cross-service coordination using Saga, CQRS, and event sourcing in realistic e-commerce workflows. It emphasizes long-running process handling, compensation, and replay-safe state.

## Key Concepts

- Saga state machine with compensations for long-running business processes
- Event sourcing and CQRS read/write separation
- Idempotency, correlation IDs, and versioning to handle retries and out-of-order events
- Compensation patterns (ReleaseInventory, RefundPayment) and failure healing
- Snapshotting to bound event stores and speed reads

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo orchestrator tick (high-level)
function onEvent(state, event) {
  if (state.orderId !== event.orderId) return state
  switch (event.type) {
    case 'CartVerified':
      return { ...state, currentStep: 'InventoryReserved' }
    case 'InventoryReserved':
      return { ...state, currentStep: 'PaymentHeld' }
    case 'PaymentFailed':
      // trigger compensations
      return { ...state, compensations: ['ReleaseInventory','OrderRollback'] }
    case 'OrderCommitted':
      return { ...state, currentStep: 'Completed' }
    default:
      return state
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you ensure idempotency across services with duplicate or replayed events?
- What strategies handle partial failures when a compensation itself fails?
- How would you design read-model projections to support fast queries while keeping the event log strictly append-only?

